> But even without seeing them, I have figured out
> that the game seems to have a lot of quest data (for example) in the memory
> all the time. Why should your rogue be interested who the heck Lord Surtur
> is and whether you can (or do) genocide him or not. (Or what's his ac etc.)

It would take substantially more memory (and effort, and time) to set
things up so that you could avoid having the quest leaders and the
associated artifacts in the executable then it would save.  It's simply
not worth it.  Note that the actual quest levels themselves are seperate
data files.

However, if you really want to save the space for the quests, comment
out MULDGN as above.

> Mind you, this was just an example. Most *rarely-used* features - LIKE 
> self-polymorphing - could be stored in separate files and read only when
> needed. I mean, why keep all those features in memory *all* the time?
> Or are there some which are not kept in memory?

Modern operating systems can perform dynamic loading of executable code.
 MS-DOS cannot; instead the programmer must go berzerk coding overlays
so that everything that should fit into memory (and would if you were
using another operating system) is broken up into pieces that will fit
into that 640K.

> Removing self-polymorph to save memory is a bad idea, since it is an
> essential part of the game concept - most game strategies would be unusable
> without self-polymorphing.

I have won without ever self-polymorphing.  It is not essential.

> BESIDES, it is present in the earlier (decent-sized) versions, which makes
> the whole argument void.

No.  According to the commentary that comes with the source,
self-polymorphing takes up a lot of space.  This plus the new features
makes NH unwieldy on your system.

Deal with it by: (a) using an older version of NetHack, (b) compiling
your own version without the features that you feel you can do without,
or (c) upgrading your system.

